{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime/turning-point bias: when the last ~5–10 days show a downside break (lower highs/lows), the model stays anchored to the prior level and overpredicts; apply a downward level shift early in the forecast (strongest from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: errors are small/slightly low in the first week during up-moves, but flip to persistent overprediction beyond ~2 weeks; introduce a negative slope correction that increases with horizon (e.g., subtract an increasing amount from day 8→30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a recent price spike to ~70–73, the model projects continued strength/plateau, while realized prices mean-revert sharply; cap post-spike forecasts and force faster mean reversion (reduce forecasts more aggressively after day ~7).",
    "Volatility compression: the model underestimates drawdown magnitude during sell-offs (true outcomes fall much more); widen downside tails by scaling negative moves (apply a multiplicative factor to forecasted declines or add extra downside shock when momentum turns negative).",
    "Lag in trend reversal: the model reacts ~1–2 weeks late to the transition from rally to sell-off; use a momentum filter (e.g., if 5d return < 0 and 10d MA rolling over) to switch to a bearish correction immediately rather than gradually."
  ]
}
